  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":41,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A library surveys 90 members about borrowing fiction (F) and non-fiction (N). 52 borrowed fiction, 38 borrowed non-fiction and 14 borrowed both.\n\n(a) Draw a two-set Venn diagram and state the value in the fiction-only region.\n(b) State the value of n(F ∪ N).\n(c) Describe the region F′ ∩ N in words and give its value.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A library surveys 90 members about borrowing fiction (F) and non-fiction (N). 52 borrowed fiction, 38 borrowed non-fiction and 14 borrowed both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Draw a two-set Venn diagram and state the value in the fiction-only region.\n(b) State the value of n(F ∪ N).\n(c) Describe the region F′ ∩ N in words and give its value."}]}]},"e":"Overlap 14, fiction only 38, non-fiction only 24; the union is 76 and 14 members borrowed neither. F′ ∩ N is the non-fiction-only region, 24.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"sets and Venn diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":41,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"B(i)","next_time_hint":"F′ ∩ N is not the same as ''neither'': it means in N, outside F. Sketch the region before computing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the value of the fiction-only region.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Subtract the 14 who borrowed both from the 52 fiction borrowers.","52 − 14."],"strand":"B(i)","answer":38,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the value of n(F ∪ N).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add the three regions inside the circles.","52 + 38 − 14 also works."],"strand":"B(ii)","answer":76,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Give the value of the region F′ ∩ N.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["F′ ∩ N is non-fiction only.","38 − 14."],"strand":"B(iii)","answer":24,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Describe F′ ∩ N in words.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Read left to right: not in F, and in N.","Which part of the non-fiction circle is outside the overlap?"],"strand":"B(iii)","accepted":["members who borrowed non-fiction but not fiction"],"keywords":[["members"],["borrowed"],["non-fiction"],["fiction"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 38. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the addition, 1 for 76. (c) 1 mark for 24. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the wording, 1 for excluding the overlap.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 38. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the addition, 1 for 76. (c) 1 mark for 24. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the wording, 1 for excluding the overlap.","response_tools":["venn","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"venn","response_guidance":"Build the diagram in the workspace; the four numbers then justify each box."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 38. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the addition, 1 for 76. (c) 1 mark for 24. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the wording, 1 for excluding the overlap."},{"n":42,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A spinner has 5 equal sectors: 2 are red, 2 are blue, 1 is gold. A game costs $4 per spin. Red pays $3, blue pays $3 and gold pays $12.\n\n(a) Write the probability of gold as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected payout per spin.\n(c) Calculate the expected loss per spin.\n(d) The organiser wants a game that is fair but keeps the $4 price. Suggest how to change the gold prize and verify your answer.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A spinner has 5 equal sectors: 2 are red, 2 are blue, 1 is gold. A game costs "},{"type":"text","text":"$4 per spin. Red pays $"},{"type":"text","text":"3, blue pays "},{"type":"text","text":"$3 and gold pays $"},{"type":"text","text":"12."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the probability of gold as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected payout per spin.\n(c) Calculate the expected loss per spin.\n(d) The organiser wants a game that is fair but keeps the "},{"type":"text","text":"$4 price. Suggest how to change the gold prize and verify your answer."}]}]},"e":"E(payout) = 0.4(3) + 0.4(3) + 0.2(12) = $4.80 against a $4 price, so the player expects to gain $0.80 per spin and the organiser to lose it. Fairness needs E(payout) = $4, so 2.4 + 0.2g = 4 gives g = $8 — the gold prize must come DOWN.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":42,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Fairness means expected payout = price of playing. Solve for the unknown prize rather than guessing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Write the probability of gold as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Every sector is equally likely, so divide the gold sectors by the total.","One gold sector out of five."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.2,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the expected payout per spin, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply each prize by its probability and add all three products.","0.4 × 3 + 0.4 × 3 + 0.2 × 12."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":4.8,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the organiser''s expected loss per spin, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Compare the expected payout with the $4 price.","4.80 − 4.00, and say whose loss it is."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":0.8,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Suggest a gold prize that makes the game fair, and verify it.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["For fairness the expected payout must equal $4.","Solve 2.4 + 0.2g = 4."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["reduce the gold prize to $8, because 0.4(3) + 0.4(3) + 0.2(8) = $4 exactly, so the expected payout equals the price"],"keywords":[["reduce"],["gold"],["prize"],["exactly"],["expected"],["payout"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three products, 1 for $4.80. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the difference, 1 for ''organiser''s loss''. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the fairness condition, 1 for $8, 1 for verification.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three products, 1 for $4.80. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the difference, 1 for ''organiser''s loss''. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the fairness condition, 1 for $8, 1 for verification.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show all three products in (b), then state in (c) whose loss you are describing."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three products, 1 for $4.80. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the difference, 1 for ''organiser''s loss''. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the fairness condition, 1 for $8, 1 for verification."},{"n":43,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A courier charges $18 for the first 5 kg and $2.40 for each additional kilogram.\n\n(a) Calculate the cost of sending a 12 kg parcel.\n(b) Calculate the average cost per kilogram for a 12 kg parcel.\n(c) A customer claims that sending two 6 kg parcels must be cheaper than one 12 kg parcel because the weight is the same. Calculate the cost of two 6 kg parcels and evaluate the claim.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A courier charges "},{"type":"text","text":"$18 for the first 5 kg and $"},{"type":"text","text":"2.40 for each additional kilogram."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the cost of sending a 12 kg parcel.\n(b) Calculate the average cost per kilogram for a 12 kg parcel.\n(c) A customer claims that sending two 6 kg parcels must be cheaper than one 12 kg parcel because the weight is the same. Calculate the cost of two 6 kg parcels and evaluate the claim."}]}]},"e":"A 12 kg parcel is 18 + 7(2.40) = $34.80, an average of $2.90 per kg. A 6 kg parcel is $20.40, so two cost $40.80 — more, because the $18 base charge applies to each parcel.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"rate","st":"rate","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":43,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"With a fixed charge plus a rate, the fixed charge is paid per parcel; splitting a consignment costs more, not less.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the number of kilograms charged at the additional rate for a 12 kg parcel.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Only the mass above the first 5 kg is charged at the extra rate.","Subtract the 5 kg already paid for from the 12 kg."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the cost of sending a 12 kg parcel, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply the 7 additional kilograms by $2.40.","Add that to the $18 base charge."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":34.8,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the average cost per kilogram for a 12 kg parcel, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Divide the total cost by the 12 kilograms.","34.80 ÷ 12."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":2.9,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"$/kg"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the cost of two 6 kg parcels and evaluate the customer''s claim.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["A 6 kg parcel pays the $18 charge plus 1 extra kilogram.","Compare $40.80 with $34.80."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["two 6 kg parcels cost 2 × $20.40 = $40.80, $6.00 more than the single parcel, so the claim is false because the fixed $18 charge is paid twice"],"keywords":[["parcels"],["cost"],["single"],["parcel"],["claim"],["false"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for $16.80 of extra charges, 1 for $34.80. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing, 1 for $2.90. (d) 3 marks: 1 for $20.40, 1 for $40.80, 1 for the fixed-charge explanation.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for $16.80 of extra charges, 1 for $34.80. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing, 1 for $2.90. (d) 3 marks: 1 for $20.40, 1 for $40.80, 1 for the fixed-charge explanation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Separate the fixed charge from the per-kilogram charge in every part; the fixed charge is what breaks the claim."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for $16.80 of extra charges, 1 for $34.80. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing, 1 for $2.90. (d) 3 marks: 1 for $20.40, 1 for $40.80, 1 for the fixed-charge explanation."},{"n":44,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A paint is mixed from blue and yellow in the ratio 3 : 5. A decorator needs 24 litres in total.\n\n(a) Calculate the litres of blue needed.\n(b) Calculate the litres of yellow needed.\n(c) The decorator has already mixed 9 litres of blue and 12 litres of yellow. Explain, with a calculation, what must be added to reach 24 litres in the correct ratio.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A paint is mixed from blue and yellow in the ratio 3 : 5. A decorator needs 24 litres in total."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the litres of blue needed.\n(b) Calculate the litres of yellow needed.\n(c) The decorator has already mixed 9 litres of blue and 12 litres of yellow. Explain, with a calculation, what must be added to reach 24 litres in the correct ratio."}]}]},"e":"3 + 5 = 8 parts, so one part is 24 ÷ 8 = 3 litres: blue 9, yellow 15. With 9 litres of blue and 12 litres of yellow already mixed, only 3 more litres of yellow are needed. Checking: 9 : 15 = 3 : 5.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":44,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Share a total by dividing it by the SUM of the ratio parts, then verify by simplifying the result.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the litres of blue needed.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["How many parts are there altogether?","Divide 24 by 8, then multiply by 3."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":9,"tolerance":0,"unit":"litres"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the litres of yellow needed.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Yellow is 5 parts.","5 × 3 litres."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":15,"tolerance":0,"unit":"litres"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain what must be added, with a calculation.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare what is already mixed with the targets 9 and 15.","Only one colour is short — which?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["add 3 litres of yellow only: the blue is already at 9 litres but the yellow is 3 litres short of 15"],"keywords":[["litres"],["yellow"],["blue"],["already"],["short"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 8 parts, 1 for 9 litres. (b) 1 mark for 15 litres. (c) 2 marks: 1 for identifying yellow as short, 1 for the 3 litres with a check.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 8 parts, 1 for 9 litres. (b) 1 mark for 15 litres. (c) 2 marks: 1 for identifying yellow as short, 1 for the 3 litres with a check.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the number of parts in (a); the same part value is used in (b)."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 8 parts, 1 for 9 litres. (b) 1 mark for 15 litres. (c) 2 marks: 1 for identifying yellow as short, 1 for the 3 litres with a check."},{"n":45,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A spinner has 10 equal sectors numbered 1 to 10. In 200 spins a student records 94 odd and 106 even results.\n\n(a) Write the theoretical probability of an odd number.\n(b) Calculate the experimental probability of an odd number in simplest form.\n(c) Compare the two and decide, with justification, whether the data suggest the spinner is biased.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A spinner has 10 equal sectors numbered 1 to 10. In 200 spins a student records 94 odd and 106 even results."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the theoretical probability of an odd number.\n(b) Calculate the experimental probability of an odd number in simplest form.\n(c) Compare the two and decide, with justification, whether the data suggest the spinner is biased."}]}]},"e":"Theoretical = 1/2; experimental = 47/100 = 0.47. The 0.03 difference is ordinary variation, so the data neither prove nor suggest bias; more spins would be needed.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":45,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"B(i)","next_time_hint":"Experimental probability approaches the theoretical value as trials increase but rarely equals it; judge bias against plausible variation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Write the theoretical probability of an odd number, in simplest form.","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["How many of 1 to 10 are odd?","Write that over 10 and simplify."],"strand":"B(i)","accepted":["1/2"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the experimental probability in simplest form.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["Relative frequency = odd results ÷ spins.","94/200, then divide by 2."],"strand":"B(ii)","accepted":["47/100"]},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Decide whether the spinner is biased, justifying your decision.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["How big is the difference between 47/100 and 1/2?","What variation would you expect by chance in 200 spins?"],"strand":"B(iii)","accepted":["no evidence of bias: 0.47 differs from 0.5 by only 0.03, which is expected sampling variation in 200 spins"],"keywords":[["evidence"],["bias"],["differs"],["expected"],["sampling"],["variation"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 1/2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 94/200, 1 for 47/100. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for a justified conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 1/2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 94/200, 1 for 47/100. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for a justified conclusion.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Give both probabilities in simplest form so they can be compared directly in (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 1/2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 94/200, 1 for 47/100. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for a justified conclusion."}]'::jsonb) as x
